## 🚀 Fully Automated Workflow (NEW)

### Two Simple Commands = Complete Automation

**Command 1: Fix Ticket**
```
fix ticket

TicketID: PROJ-1234
Type: Bug|Enhancement|CR|Analysis
Description: <issue description>
```

**Command 2: Fix ADA Issues**
```
/fixada-file C:\BrowserStackReport\scan.csv
/fixada-folder C:\BrowserStackReport\
/fixada-results https://accessibility.browserstack.com/report/123
/fixada-preventive C:\path\to\code\
```

### What Happens Automatically

**Ticket Workflow:**
```
1. ✅ Fetch the ticket from the ticket tracker (if accessible)
2. ✅ Load domain knowledge from .ai/knowledge-base.md
3. ✅ Analyze scope, risk, and impact
--- TYPE GATE (non-negotiable — evaluated immediately after step 3) ---

Type = Analysis:
  → Deliver written analysis report in chat ONLY
  → STOP — no branch, no code, no build, no push, no commit
  → Do NOT continue to steps 4–14 under any circumstances
  → See .ai/behavior/setting.md Section 2 (Ticket Type Risk Posture — Analysis row) and
    .ai/behavior/decision-rules.md STOP condition #14 for the Analysis posture
  → Mode: "Ask" or "Plan" chat mode is sufficient — Analysis is read-only (no edits/build/push), so
    Ask/Plan mode's restricted toolset can complete it. Agent mode also works.

Type = Bug:
  → Continue to step 4
  → Branch naming: per {{BRANCH_PREFIX_TABLE}}, from `{{BASE_BRANCHES}}`
  → Build scope: Module solution only (e.g. {{SOLUTION_FILES}})
  → Full {{SOLUTION_FILES}} build only if shared libs touched
  → STOP if more than 3 files need to change — confirm scope first
  → Minimum 1 reviewer required on PR
  → Mode: Requires "Agent" chat mode (or the Ticket Processor agent) — Plan mode cannot
    create branches, edit files, run builds, or push

Type = Enhancement:
  → Continue to step 4
  → Branch naming: per {{BRANCH_PREFIX_TABLE}}, from `{{BASE_BRANCHES}}`
  → Build scope: Full {{SOLUTION_FILES}} mandatory
  → Minimum 1 reviewer required on PR
  → Flag any cross-module impact before implementing
  → Mode: Requires "Agent" chat mode (or the Ticket Processor agent) — same reason as above

Type = CR (Change Request):
  → Continue to step 4
  → Branch naming: per {{BRANCH_PREFIX_TABLE}}, from `{{BASE_BRANCHES}}`
  → Build scope: Full {{SOLUTION_FILES}} mandatory
  → Minimum 1 reviewer required on PR
  → STOP if shared library change needed — require team lead sign-off first
  → Show full regression testing checklist before commit
  → Mode: Requires "Agent" chat mode (or the Ticket Processor agent) — same reason as above

----------------------------------------------------------------------
4. ⏸️  Present analysis → Wait for your confirmation
5. ✅ Check current branch → if {{BASE_BRANCHES}}: git pull + ask branch strategy (A: stay on {{BASE_BRANCHES}} / B: create new branch)
6. ✅ Implement fix using domain patterns
7. ✅ Validate build passes
8. ✅ Show testing checklist in chat
9. ⏸️  Ask commit confirmation → Wait for "yes" or "y" → commit code
10. ⏸️ Show changes summary → Wait for "PUSH" approval
11. ⏸️ Ask for reviewer emails
12. ✅ Push branch
13. ✅ Create Pull Request
14. ✅ Provide instructions to link PR to the ticket
```

**ADA Workflow:**
```
1. ✅ Load WCAG patterns from .ai/governance/wcag-fix-patterns.md
2. ✅ Parse violations from CSV/folder/URL
3. ✅ Triage by severity
4. ⏸️  Ask branch strategy → Wait for your choice
5. ✅ Apply fixes using 3-tier priority
6. ✅ Validate build passes
7. ⏸️  Show changes → Wait for "PUSH" approval
8. ✅ Push branch
9. ✅ Create Pull Request
```

### How to Use

**For Ticket Fixes:**
```
fix ticket

TicketID: PROJ-1234
Type: Bug
Description: <your description>
```

**For ADA Fixes (use the specific command for your source):**
```
/fixada-file C:\Reports\scan.csv          ← single file
/fixada-folder C:\Reports\               ← whole folder
/fixada-results https://browserstack...  ← URL
/fixada-preventive C:\path\to\code\      ← pre-scan check
```

**You only need to:**
- ✅ Confirm the pre-code analysis (Ticket) OR branch strategy (ADA)
- ✅ Choose branch strategy: A (stay on {{BASE_BRANCHES}}) or B (create new branch)
- ✅ Type "yes" or "y" when ready to commit
- ✅ Provide reviewer email addresses when prompted
- ✅ Type "PUSH" when ready to push

**Everything else is automated** — no manual branching, no manual commits, no manual PR creation.

### Which Chat Mode to Use

| Ticket `Type` | Chat mode | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Analysis` | **Ask mode**, **Plan mode**, or Agent mode | Read-only — no branch/edit/build/push, so Ask/Plan mode's restricted toolset is enough |
| `Bug` / `Enhancement` / `CR` | **Agent mode** (or the `Ticket Processor` agent) | Requires file edits, `run_in_terminal` (build/git), and subagent calls — tools Plan mode does not expose |
| ADA fixes (`/fixada-*`) | **Agent mode** (or `Ticket Processor` agent) | Same reason — file edits, build, push |

If you start a Bug/Enhancement/CR ticket in Plan mode, it will only complete the pre-code analysis and then stop — this is expected, not a bug. Switch to Agent mode to run steps 4–14.

### Required Model — Strictly Enforced

Whenever the `fix ticket` command (or `/auto-fix`, `/fixada-*`) is used, the workflow MUST run on
**{{REQUIRED_MODEL_LIST}}** — no other model. This is enforced via the `model:`
frontmatter field (with fallback array) on:
- [`.github/prompts/fix-ticket.prompt.md`](.github/prompts/fix-ticket.prompt.md)
- [`.github/prompts/auto-fix.prompt.md`](.github/prompts/auto-fix.prompt.md)
- [`.github/agents/ticket-processor.agent.md`](.github/agents/ticket-processor.agent.md)
- [`.github/agents/domain-engineer.agent.md`](.github/agents/domain-engineer.agent.md) (invoked as a subagent during implementation)

Do not manually switch to a different model mid-workflow for these commands.

> **VS Code vs CLI:** The `model:` frontmatter override is confirmed to work in VS Code (and
> JetBrains/Eclipse/Xcode). GitHub Copilot CLI may not honor it the same way — the CLI session's
> currently active model takes priority. When running `fix ticket` from the CLI, explicitly set
> the model for the session first (`copilot --model <model-name>` at launch, or the `/model`
> command inside a session) so it matches {{REQUIRED_MODEL_LIST}} before invoking the workflow.

### Running `fix ticket` from GitHub Copilot CLI

The `/fix-ticket` and `/auto-fix` **slash commands** only exist in VS Code — Copilot CLI does not
discover `.github/prompts/*.prompt.md` files at all. The underlying automation
(`.github/agents/ticket-processor.agent.md`) IS read by the CLI (repository-level custom agents
live in `.github/agents`, same path for both), so the full workflow still works — it's just
invoked differently:

```
# Option 1: call out to the agent directly in a prompt (CLI infers the agent)
Use the Ticket Processor agent to fix this ticket:
TicketID: PROJ-1234
Type: Bug
Description: <issue description>

# Option 2: select it interactively
/agent
# then choose "Ticket Processor" from the list

# Option 3: pass it as a command-line flag
copilot --agent="Ticket Processor" --prompt "TicketID: PROJ-1234
Type: Bug
Description: <issue description>"
```

Two things to set up once before relying on this in the CLI:
1. **Model** — set it explicitly for the session (see note above); frontmatter enforcement is not
   guaranteed outside VS Code/IDEs.
2. **{{SCM_PLATFORM}} MCP server** — the CLI keeps its own MCP registry, separate from `.vscode/mcp.json`
   (default `~/.copilot/mcp-config.json`, or the `COPILOT_HOME` directory). Register the {{SCM_PLATFORM}}
   server there too (`copilot mcp add --transport http <server-name> <url>` or `/mcp add`) so STEP 9
   (PR creation) can run from the CLI; otherwise the agent falls back to reporting the pushed
   branch and asking you to open the PR manually in {{SCM_PLATFORM}}.

Everything else — `.ai/knowledge-base.md`, `.ai/governance/*`, `.ai/behavior/*`, `AGENTS.md`, and
`.github/copilot-instructions.md` — is plain Markdown read identically by both VS Code and the CLI,
so ticket-type rules, STOP/FLAG logic, build scope, and reviewer counts behave the same either way.

### Available Agents

- **Ticket Processor** — Fully automated ticket-to-PR workflow
- **Domain Engineer** — Domain-aware code implementation and review

